INT. PLANETARIUM CAFE - CLOSED - NIGHT

Chairs upturned on tables. A cooler humming. A faded mural of the solar system peeling above the counter.

PHILOMENA CROSS, 40s, in a heavy knit sweater, sits very still at a window booth, a cold cup untouched before her. Across from her, GRADY, 30s, shirtsleeves rolled, taps a spoon against his saucer. On a stool at the counter, TUCKER, 30s, in a tweed jacket, spins slowly one way, then the other.

Nobody says anything. The spoon taps.

He glances at her. She glances away. He taps once more. Stops.

Tucker lets the stool stop turning. He rests his elbows on the counter, studies the mural.

TUCKER
Jupiter's too small.

GRADY
It's accurate.

TUCKER
It's a third of the size it ought to be.

GRADY
Ought to be where?

TUCKER
In the picture.

Philomena slides her cup an inch toward the center of the table. Grady's eyes follow it.

GRADY
You want that reheated?

PHILOMENA
No.

GRADY
You'll catch cold.

PHILOMENA
I'll manage.

Grady scrapes his chair back. Walks behind the counter. Tucker watches him pass, lets his hand drift to the edge of a drawer, and closes it again.

He turns his head, slow, toward Philomena. She is already looking at him. He stays still. She doesn't blink.

Grady comes back with a pot of coffee, a fresh cup. He pours. The smell of it fills the room.

GRADY
Drink it. Please.

She holds the cup but doesn't lift it. Steam curls up past her face.

TUCKER
(not looking)
You don't have to say it.

PHILOMENA
I wasn't going to.

TUCKER
Good.

Grady sets down the pot. Rests one hand on the back of Tucker's stool, the other flat on the table, close to Philomena's. They sit like that, three of them, around a thing nobody names, until the cooler cycles off and the silence turns total.
